VIG = "A 27-year-old motorcyclist without a helmet is brought to a district hospital in Bihar 90 minutes after colliding with a tractor. He was unconscious for about 2 minutes, has vomited twice and smells of alcohol. GCS is 12 (E3 V4 M5), pupils are equal and reactive, BP 132/84 mmHg, pulse 96/min and SpO2 97% on air. There is a boggy scalp swelling over the left temple. The nearest CT scanner is 70 km away."

EXP = [
 explain("pathophysiology", "Primary and secondary brain injury", "Why does early care change outcome when the impact has already happened?",
  "The damage at impact — contusion, axonal shearing and haematoma — cannot be undone, but much of the disability after head injury comes from secondary injury in the hours that follow. Hypoxia, hypotension, hypercarbia, hypoglycaemia, seizures and an expanding haematoma all reduce oxygen delivery to brain tissue that is already vulnerable. A single episode of hypotension or hypoxia during transfer is associated with worse outcome. This is why the workflow insists on stabilising airway, breathing and circulation before transfer, and why a falling GCS, a widening pupil or the Cushing response (bradycardia with hypertension) are emergencies: they signal rising intracranial pressure and impending herniation. Tranexamic acid works on the same principle — it limits haematoma growth when given within 3 hours."),
 explain("signs-symptoms", "Reading the GCS and the red flags", "How do you recognise the patient who is getting worse?",
  "Record the GCS by its components (eye, verbal, motor), because a change in motor score is the most reliable sign of deterioration. Mild injury is GCS 13–15, moderate 9–12 and severe 3–8. A drop of even one or two points, a new focal deficit, unequal pupils, persistent vomiting, worsening headache or restlessness all mean the brain is under threat. Alcohol is common in Indian road-traffic injuries and is a trap: an intoxicated patient with a low GCS must be scanned and reassessed, never assumed to be 'just drunk'. Always look for associated injuries to the chest, abdomen, pelvis and long bones, because blood loss elsewhere causes the hypotension that harms the brain."),
 explain("investigations", "Who needs a CT and when", "Which patients need imaging, and what does the scan decide?",
  "Non-contrast CT of the head with cervical spine imaging is the key test and should be done as soon as possible in anyone who is not completely asymptomatic. The workflow lists definite indications: GCS below 15, any loss of consciousness, suspected skull fracture, post-traumatic seizure, a focal deficit, persistent vomiting or amnesia. The CT answers three questions: is there a lesion that needs surgery (an extradural haematoma above 30 cc, or a subdural above 10 mm with midline shift above 5 mm and deterioration); does the patient need admission; and is there bleeding that makes tranexamic acid worthwhile. Where the scanner is far away, stabilise first, give tranexamic acid if within 3 hours and GCS is 12 or less, and transfer with monitoring."),
 explain("management", "Protecting the brain until definitive care", "What are the priorities for a moderate or severe head injury?",
  "Secure the airway (by standard practice, intubate if GCS is 8 or less or the airway is at risk), keep oxygen saturation and blood pressure normal — in severe injury, systolic BP of at least 100–110 mmHg depending on age — and elevate the head of the bed. Give tranexamic acid 1 g over 10 minutes then 1 g over 8 hours within 3 hours for GCS 12 or less or a bleed on CT. Signs of rising ICP with an adequate BP are treated with mannitol or 3% saline as a bridge to surgery; prolonged hyperventilation and steroids do not help. Phenytoin or levetiracetam is used for post-traumatic seizures. Treat pain with paracetamol and carefully titrated morphine, checking GCS and pupils before each opioid dose so that sedation does not hide deterioration.")]

QS = [
 bq("q1", "A 27-year-old man arrives 90 minutes after a motorcycle crash. He was unconscious for 2 minutes and has GCS 12 with equal pupils. BP is 132/84 mmHg and SpO2 97%. The nearest CT scanner is 70 km away, and there is no other bleeding.", "Which is the most appropriate step before transfer?",
  [("Give tranexamic acid 1 g IV over 10 minutes, then 1 g over 8 hours", "Correct. GCS 12 within 3 hours of injury meets CRASH-3 criteria; the infusion can run during transfer."),
   ("Give dexamethasone to reduce brain swelling", "Steroids do not improve outcome or lower ICP in head injury (ICMR STW; BTF)."),
   ("Give mannitol 20% 200 mL", "Mannitol is for clinical signs of raised ICP with systolic BP above 90 mmHg; he has none."),
   ("Wait until the CT result before any treatment", "Tranexamic acid only helps within 3 hours; waiting for a distant CT would lose the window."),
   ("Hyperventilate to a PaCO2 of 25 mmHg", "Prolonged hyperventilation to 25 mmHg or less is not recommended (BTF).")],
  "A", "Recognise the indication and time window for tranexamic acid in TBI.", "GCS 12 or less or bleed on CT, within 3 hours: tranexamic acid 1 g + 1 g."),
 bq("q2", "A 45-year-old woman fell from a ladder. GCS was 14 on arrival and is now 10 an hour later, with a dilating right pupil. BP 168/90 mmHg, pulse 54/min. CT shows a right extradural haematoma of 45 cc with midline shift.", "What is the best next step?",
  [("Admit for hourly neurological observations", "Observation alone is unsafe: she has a surgical lesion and signs of herniation."),
   ("Urgent neurosurgical evacuation, with mannitol as a bridge", "Correct. An extradural haematoma above 30 cc needs surgery (ICMR STW); mannitol is appropriate with systolic BP above 90 mmHg and red flags."),
   ("Start phenytoin and repeat CT in 24 hours", "Seizure prophylaxis does not treat an expanding haematoma; delay risks death."),
   ("Treat the bradycardia with atropine", "Bradycardia with hypertension is the Cushing response to raised ICP; the treatment is decompression."),
   ("Lumbar puncture to measure pressure", "Lumbar puncture is dangerous with a mass lesion and raised ICP (risk of herniation).")],
  "B", "Identify a surgical lesion and the signs of herniation.", "Extradural haematoma above 30 cc, or deterioration with shift: neurosurgery now."),
 bq("q3", "A 35-year-old man was assaulted and is drowsy, smells strongly of alcohol, and has GCS 13. He has a 4 cm scalp laceration over the occiput but no vomiting, and his pupils are equal. His friends say he is 'just drunk', that he often drinks this much, and want to take him home tonight.", "What is the most appropriate management?",
  [("Allow discharge with friends because the GCS is above 12", "GCS below 15 after head injury is a CT indication (ICMR STW); alcohol does not explain it safely."),
   ("Observe until sober, then decide on imaging", "Waiting delays the diagnosis of an intracranial bleed; intoxication is an admission criterion in itself."),
   ("CT head with cervical spine imaging and admission for observation", "Correct. GCS below 15 mandates CT, and intoxication is an admission criterion (ICMR STW)."),
   ("Give naloxone and reassess", "Naloxone has no role unless opioid toxicity is suspected; it does not exclude brain injury."),
   ("Skull X-ray only", "Skull X-ray does not show intracranial bleeding; CT is the test.")],
  "C", "Do not attribute a low GCS to alcohol after head injury.", "Head injury + intoxication: CT and admit; never assume 'just drunk'.")]

